#!lm
MODEL=litellm_proxy/anthropic/Claude Opus 5
CONTEXT=urn:cts:latinLit:phi0474.phi016.omar:6.6.t97-urn:cts:latinLit:phi0474.phi016.omar:6.6.t130
REASONING=The passage has two coordinated independent verbs, **voluit** and **impetravit**, joined by the enclitic **-que** (attached to "id", the direct object of impetravit, but functionally joining the two verbs). Each gets its own 'root'/'unit verb' relation. "quae cum esset civitas aequissimo iure ac foedere": a *cum*-clause subordinate to **voluit**; **esset** is its dependent verb (linking), with **quae** as subject and **civitas** as predicate nominative. The antecedent of **quae** (Heraclea) lies outside the excerpt, so no 'relative pronoun' link is possible; **quae** therefore takes only its ordinary 'subject' relation. **aequissimo iure ac foedere** is an ablative of description attached to **civitas** (both ablatives relate to that noun; **ac** joins them). "ascribi se in eam civitatem voluit": **ascribi** completes the sense of **voluit** — I annotate it as a complementary infinitive (the instructions explicitly name *volo* for that label). AMBIGUITY: because the infinitive has its own expressed accusative subject **se**, this could defensibly be analysed instead as an object clause / indirect statement anchored at **ascribi** (which would make **ascribi** its own verbal expression with syntactic type 'indirect statement'). I chose the complementary reading on the strength of the explicit *volo* guidance, but note the alternative. Either way, **se** is the subject of **ascribi**, and **in eam civitatem** modifies **ascribi** adverbially. "cum ipse per se dignus putaretur, tum ... impetravit": the correlative *cum ... tum* ("both ... and"); **cum** is the subordinating conjunction pointing at **impetravit**, **putaretur** is its dependent verb, classified as a linking verb since the passive of *puto* links **ipse** to the predicate adjective **dignus**. **tum** is treated as an adverb modifying **impetravit**. **auctoritate et gratia** are ablatives of means with **impetravit**, joined by **et**; **Luculli** is a genitive depending on the nearer noun **gratia**. **ab Heracliensibus** is not an agent construction (impetravit is active), so **ab** is annotated as an ordinary preposition modifying the verb adverbially, with **Heracliensibus** as its object.

